Z||000004||Taijiguy||04-13-2001||05:01 PM||||Don't want a carbed engine? Eh-Hem!And just why not?<BR>~Taiji<BR>(Proud owner of a highly competitive CARBED 2.8 GT) ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-13-2001||06:12 PM||||I like carbs, but well,,, in areas with emissions inspections they often won't pass in cars that didn't come with them. That and alot of people can't tune them right. <P>A truely good running carb motor is the exception. not the norm. It's gotten to the point where the number of profesional mechanics who can do this job properly is dropping steadily.<P>If the carb motor isn't 100% to start with it can cost even more than fixing EFI. New carbs cost hundreds of dollars. The OE one for my 86 caravan is over $500. Most OE and after markets for GM are in that range and higher. Unfotunatly, as old as many carbs are now, they are all too often worn beyond what a rebuild kit can do for them.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||JR||04-15-2001||08:09 PM||||Taiji:<BR>What carb and intake manifold are you using?  I've got an Offenhauser intake with a Holley 490 double pumper.  Engines putting out 270 hp, and the carb is tuned such that I can pull smoothly without stumble from 1000rpm to 8000rpm. Have equal length headers and highly modified internals including titanium valves, weiseico forged pistons,pbolished balanced and knife-edged crank mmoly pushrods, crane roller rockers and crower mechanical cam. Could never get that kind of performance using FI without spending thousands more for a custom system.  Carbs rule for pushrods for power<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B>Don't want a carbed engine? Eh-Hem!And just why not?<BR>~Taiji<BR>(Proud owner of a highly competitive CARBED 2.8 GT) </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>85GT (daily driver 120,000 miles)<BR>88GT (highly modified sunday driver)||||reg||1
